{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And they spoke to him all the words of Joseph.</b> Rashi explained: a sign he gave them when he parted from him — he was engaged in the passage of the egla arufa. And one needs an explanation according to this. This is what it means to say: when he parted from his father, his father escorted him, as written: וישלחהו — which is the language of escort, like וישלחו written regarding Abraham, which is translated ואלויאו. And Joseph said to him: turn back. And Jacob said to him: My son, a great escort is this, for upon it an additional passage was added in the Torah, as written: ידינו לא שפכה. For would it enter your mind that a court spills blood? Rather, they did not see him and released him without escort — but if they saw him and did not escort him, it is accounted against them as though they spilled his blood, and that is the passage of egla arufa, which he was engaged in. And some say: he was engaged in a wagon that pulls in a cart. And it is difficult for both explanations to explain עגלות as the language of עגלה. Therefore it appears correct to explain it as the passage of the wagons of the Tabernacle, as written: שש עגלות צב." "<b>And he saw the wagons, and the spirit of Jacob their father revived.</b> This means: when he saw the wagons, his spirit revived, and he believed. For beforehand, Joseph had commanded that they not take wagons out of the land of Egypt, and now Pharaoh said: ואתה צויתה זאת עשו — take wagons, which others are not permitted to take out. Therefore, when he saw the wagons, he believed and said: surely this is from the mouth of the king."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The phrase והכי רוצה לומר introduces the author's own explanation of Rashi's comment about the egla arufa sign."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:07:12.190794366Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}